{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019ZLgReR6NbfijUAiGFxYAD","type":"tool_result","content":"3390\t                operations. Delegates to the mage-claude-orchestrator library, which drives Claude\n3391\t                agents for code generation. This is the boundary between the human-guided spec\n3392\t                phase and the automated synthesis phase.\n3393\t              capabilities:\n3394\t                - mage build compiles all binaries\n3395\t                - mage lint runs golangci-lint\n3396\t                - mage stats:loc prints LOC and doc word counts\n3397\t                - mage analyze runs cross-artifact consistency checks\n3398\t                - mage init initializes the project\n3399\t                - mage credentials extracts Claude credentials from macOS Keychain\n3400\t                - mage cobbler:measure and mage cobbler:stitch run the orchestrator generation cycle\n3401\t              references:\n3402\t                - magefiles/\n3403\t        design_decisions:\n3404\t            - id: 1\n3405\t              title: C source as the specification source of truth\n3406\t              decision: |\n3407\t                We derive specs from the C source code of coreutils, moreutils, grep, and findutils\n3408\t                rather than from man pages or documentation alone.\n3409\t              benefits:\n3410\t                - Captures edge cases and flag interactions not documented in man pages\n3411\t                - Eliminates ambiguity about intended behavior\n3412\t                - Enables completeness verification against the actual implementation\n3413\t              alternatives_rejected:\n3414\t                - 'Man-page-only specs: insufficient detail for edge cases and exit codes'\n3415\t                - 'Behavioral-only extraction: slower, misses implementation intent for unexposed paths'\n3416\t            - id: 2\n3417\t              title: Differential testing as the primary verification mechanism\n3418\t              decision: |\n3419\t                We verify Go implementations by running them side by side with the Homebrew GNU\n3420\t                reference binary and comparing outputs, not by writing unit tests against expected\n3421\t                constants.\n3422\t              benefits:\n3423\t                - Tests against the actual reference, not a human's interpretation of it\n3424\t                - Discovers regressions automatically when spec or code changes\n3425\t                - Scales to any number of inputs without manual expected-output authoring\n3426\t              alternatives_rejected:\n3427\t                - 'Unit tests with hardcoded expectations: brittle, based on documentation assumptions'\n3428\t                - 'Integration tests against a fixed corpus: does not verify against live GNU behavior'\n3429\t            - id: 3\n3430\t              title: Shared pkg/ library for Unix primitives\n3431\t              decision: |\n3432\t                Common logic (syscalls, formatting, I/O patterns) is promoted to pkg/ rather than\n3433\t                duplicated across cmd/ packages.\n3434\t              benefits:\n3435\t                - Reduces duplication and inconsistency between utilities\n3436\t                - Builds a reusable Go library of Unix primitives\n3437\t                - Makes each cmd/ package focused on utility-specific logic\n3438\t              alternatives_rejected:\n3439\t                - 'Per-utility internal packages: promotes duplication across cmd/'\n3440\t                - 'External formatting libraries: reduces control, increases supply-chain surface'\n3441\t            - id: 4\n3442\t              title: Homebrew GNU binaries as reference, not macOS built-ins\n3443\t              decision: |\n3444\t                On macOS we use gls, gts, and other g-prefixed Homebrew binaries as the reference,\n3445\t                not the BSD-derived macOS built-ins.\n3446\t              benefits:\n3447\t                - GNU behavior is well-defined and derived from the same C source we clone\n3448\t                - Avoids testing against BSD variants with different flag sets and semantics\n3449\t                - Homebrew binaries are the same version as the cloned source\n3450\t              alternatives_rejected:\n3451\t                - 'macOS built-ins: BSD-derived, significant flag and behavior differences from GNU'\n3452\t                - 'Building from source ourselves: adds complexity without benefit'\n3453\t            - id: 5\n3454\t              title: Human-guided specification, automated synthesis\n3455\t              decision: |\n3456\t                We split responsibility at the spec boundary. The human operator owns the SRD\n3457\t                via make-work and do-work skills. The mage-claude-orchestrator owns code generation\n3458\t                and testing from that point forward. The operator does not write Go; the orchestrator\n3459\t                does not write SRDs.\n3460\t              benefits:\n3461\t                - Isolates the experiment variable so orchestrator quality is evaluated independently of spec quality\n3462\t                - Human judgment is applied where it adds most value (behavioral analysis of C source)\n3463\t                - Orchestrator failure modes are observable without confounding spec quality\n3464\t              alternatives_rejected:\n3465\t                - Fully automated pipeline (orchestrator writes specs too) conflates two failure modes\n3466\t                - Fully manual pipeline (human writes Go) does not test the orchestrator at all\n3467\t            - id: 7\n3468\t              title: Generation scope excludes scaffolded and human-authored paths\n3469\t              decision: |\n3470\t                The code generation pipeline (measure + stitch) must not propose or execute\n3471\t                tasks for the following paths. These are either scaffolded by cobbler-scaffold\n3472\t                or human-authored content that the pipeline reads but does not write.\n3473\t\n3474\t                magefiles/ -- provided by cobbler-scaffold's orchestrator library.\n3475\t                orchestrator.go is auto-generated. The only code generation target in\n3476\t                srd011-magefiles is cmd/version, which lives in cmd/version/.\n3477\t\n3478\t                configuration.yaml -- consumed by cobbler-scaffold. Not a generation target.\n3479\t\n3480\t                .cobbler/ -- scaffold working directory. Not a generation target.\n3481\t\n3482\t                .claude/ -- Claude Code configuration. Not a generation target.\n3483\t\n3484\t                docs/ -- specifications are human-authored. The pipeline reads docs/ as input\n3485\t                but does not write to it.\n3486\t\n3487\t                The generation pipeline targets only cmd/ and pkg/ directories.\n3488\t              benefits:\n3489\t                - Prevents measure from proposing tasks for scaffolded infrastructure"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"566fb101-3845-4dae-83a6-ea469666fae6","uuid":"a4e6edfe-8359-4796-a891-26c915670600","timestamp":"2026-05-15T14:18:15.244Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5269/.cobbler/history/2026-05-15-10-06-13-stitch-prompt.yaml","content":"                operations. Delegates to the mage-claude-orchestrator library, which drives Claude\n                agents for code generation. This is the boundary between the human-guided spec\n                phase and the automated synthesis phase.\n              capabilities:\n                - mage build compiles all binaries\n                - mage lint runs golangci-lint\n                - mage stats:loc prints LOC and doc word counts\n                - mage analyze runs cross-artifact consistency checks\n                - mage init initializes the project\n                - mage credentials extracts Claude credentials from macOS Keychain\n                - mage cobbler:measure and mage cobbler:stitch run the orchestrator generation cycle\n              references:\n                - magefiles/\n        design_decisions:\n            - id: 1\n              title: C source as the specification source of truth\n              decision: |\n                We derive specs from the C source code of coreutils, moreutils, grep, and findutils\n                rather than from man pages or documentation alone.\n              benefits:\n                - Captures edge cases and flag interactions not documented in man pages\n                - Eliminates ambiguity about intended behavior\n                - Enables completeness verification against the actual implementation\n              alternatives_rejected:\n                - 'Man-page-only specs: insufficient detail for edge cases and exit codes'\n                - 'Behavioral-only extraction: slower, misses implementation intent for unexposed paths'\n            - id: 2\n              title: Differential testing as the primary verification mechanism\n              decision: |\n                We verify Go implementations by running them side by side with the Homebrew GNU\n                reference binary and comparing outputs, not by writing unit tests against expected\n                constants.\n              benefits:\n                - Tests against the actual reference, not a human's interpretation of it\n                - Discovers regressions automatically when spec or code changes\n                - Scales to any number of inputs without manual expected-output authoring\n              alternatives_rejected:\n                - 'Unit tests with hardcoded expectations: brittle, based on documentation assumptions'\n                - 'Integration tests against a fixed corpus: does not verify against live GNU behavior'\n            - id: 3\n              title: Shared pkg/ library for Unix primitives\n              decision: |\n                Common logic (syscalls, formatting, I/O patterns) is promoted to pkg/ rather than\n                duplicated across cmd/ packages.\n              benefits:\n                - Reduces duplication and inconsistency between utilities\n                - Builds a reusable Go library of Unix primitives\n                - Makes each cmd/ package focused on utility-specific logic\n              alternatives_rejected:\n                - 'Per-utility internal packages: promotes duplication across cmd/'\n                - 'External formatting libraries: reduces control, increases supply-chain surface'\n            - id: 4\n              title: Homebrew GNU binaries as reference, not macOS built-ins\n              decision: |\n                On macOS we use gls, gts, and other g-prefixed Homebrew binaries as the reference,\n                not the BSD-derived macOS built-ins.\n              benefits:\n                - GNU behavior is well-defined and derived from the same C source we clone\n                - Avoids testing against BSD variants with different flag sets and semantics\n                - Homebrew binaries are the same version as the cloned source\n              alternatives_rejected:\n                - 'macOS built-ins: BSD-derived, significant flag and behavior differences from GNU'\n                - 'Building from source ourselves: adds complexity without benefit'\n            - id: 5\n              title: Human-guided specification, automated synthesis\n              decision: |\n                We split responsibility at the spec boundary. The human operator owns the SRD\n                via make-work and do-work skills. The mage-claude-orchestrator owns code generation\n                and testing from that point forward. The operator does not write Go; the orchestrator\n                does not write SRDs.\n              benefits:\n                - Isolates the experiment variable so orchestrator quality is evaluated independently of spec quality\n                - Human judgment is applied where it adds most value (behavioral analysis of C source)\n                - Orchestrator failure modes are observable without confounding spec quality\n              alternatives_rejected:\n                - Fully automated pipeline (orchestrator writes specs too) conflates two failure modes\n                - Fully manual pipeline (human writes Go) does not test the orchestrator at all\n            - id: 7\n              title: Generation scope excludes scaffolded and human-authored paths\n              decision: |\n                The code generation pipeline (measure + stitch) must not propose or execute\n                tasks for the following paths. These are either scaffolded by cobbler-scaffold\n                or human-authored content that the pipeline reads but does not write.\n\n                magefiles/ -- provided by cobbler-scaffold's orchestrator library.\n                orchestrator.go is auto-generated. The only code generation target in\n                srd011-magefiles is cmd/version, which lives in cmd/version/.\n\n                configuration.yaml -- consumed by cobbler-scaffold. Not a generation target.\n\n                .cobbler/ -- scaffold working directory. Not a generation target.\n\n                .claude/ -- Claude Code configuration. Not a generation target.\n\n                docs/ -- specifications are human-authored. The pipeline reads docs/ as input\n                but does not write to it.\n\n                The generation pipeline targets only cmd/ and pkg/ directories.\n              benefits:\n                - Prevents measure from proposing tasks for scaffolded infrastructure","numLines":100,"startLine":3390,"totalLines":8696}}}
